95 year-old mariano oginski often thinks  back to his days on the western front he  was 24 when he joined a Polish Armored  Division he was sent to Britain to help  liberate northern France but when the  Allies landed in Normandy suave in skis  division had to stay behind to wait for  equipment from the US  when the first troops arrived in France  we were really envious we'd spent a lot  of time training for that operation so  we were keen to join the Allies in  Normandy as soon as possible and they  were waiting for us to our division was  supposed to be on the front line and  move things forward quickly the Polish  division reached France at the end of  July they had the crucial role of  closing off an escape route for German  soldiers at fundation woth after three  days and nights of intense fighting they  succeeded thousands of German soldiers  were surrounded by Allied forces when we  were given the command to fire I look  down the hill and saw endless numbers of  German soldiers please forgive me I  don't really want to talk about it we  just shot into the masses there were  masses of Germans that battle led to the  liberation of France so well Vince key  wanted to press on and liberate his  homeland but he wasn't able to return  until after the war Poland was then  under Soviet control many of his  comrades emigrated this is one of my  comrades we were in a tank together he  emigrated to Canada sadly he died  recently his wife died too we stayed in  contact for a long time and visited each  other several times so Vince key will be  attending this year's commemorations in  Normandy where he hopes to meet with  both former friends and foes I'd be  happy to meet German veterans there too  I don't believe in living in the past we  must not forget but we need to look to  the future too I will shake hands with  the Germans and wish them all the best  at the age of 95 Marianne suave in skeet  will be the oldest better  and from Poland he'll return to the  scene of his worst battle an area that  he now hopes will be a place of  reconciliation
 

